 "instructions": "Translate every \"en\" string into Simplified Chinese (zh-Hans), game-guide register. Follow glossary.json strictly. Keep ⟦0⟧-style placeholders, numbers, units and wiki markup exactly. Return only {\"items\":[{\"k\":\"...\",\"zh\":\"...\"}]} with the same k values.",
 "units": [
  {
   "k": "1kk4kov",
   "en": "First, one should decide one thing: if they want to be cooperative with the Left Opposition, or if they wish to save the generals Stalin historically purges. This will determine focus order. Regardless, the first 3 focuses one should take are ⟦0⟧ ⟦1⟧, and ⟦2⟧ - for the political power you get, either hire Mikhail Kalinin or take Improved Worker Conditions for a much needed stability boost (note that until Bukharin is in charge, you will not be able to earn much political power - although you can somewhat offset this by infiltrating the civilian governments of other countries; if playing on ahistorical, there's a chance Turkey might back down when you mobilize the armed forces in response to them remilitarizing the Bosporus, thus giving you some stability and political power). After that, take ⟦3⟧ and ⟦4⟧, then go up to Partial Mobilization, and round it off with ⟦5⟧ - while you might think that ⟦6⟧ is a good idea if not working with the Left Opposition, doing so will boost Paranoia, so it's best avoided. However, if you want to attack another country, consider holding off to see if you can go for War Economy instead of Partial Mobilization after justifying.",
   "frags": 7
  },
  {
   "k": "6dfbvw",
   "en": "The Path of Marx-Leninism,"
  },
  {
   "k": "o6nggf",
   "en": "Infiltrate the NKVD"
  },
  {
   "k": "3gt9dc",
   "en": "Gain Support from Party Members"
  },
  {
   "k": "m8bg9r",
   "en": "Divert Attention Towards the Military"
  },
  {
   "k": "hlv6z5",
   "en": "Eliminate the Left"
  },
  {
   "k": "i4kpz",
   "en": "While this may seem like you will not finish the latter before the ⟦0⟧ finishes, the game will prioritize the player's focus as long as it completes on the same day (and both of the latter focuses will bypass the Terror Center). Additionally, if we take the focus that bypasses the aforementioned Center focus before, Stalin will merely speed up his focuses, whereas if we let it play out but not finish, he'll be on the same track but we'll be closer. While it might seem ideal to purge army officers because of how many you have, if you need to lower paranoia, focus on purging the navy - while you have more generals, the navy is not as important to the Soviet Union as it is to the British or Japanese. Generate new admirals as needed, and also forge production reports if you don't have the option to purge an admiral. Only purge officers if you have no other choice.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "np6nsk",
   "en": "Zinovyevite Terror Center"
  },
  {
   "k": "18d94ro",
   "en": "It should be noted that while Beria will be the one who can lock off the bloodless coup, if Yagoda is purged, he will be replaced with Nikolay Yezhov - this is both good and bad, since while doing so will significantly lower paranoia, Yezhov will generate more paranoia on a weekly basis than Yagoda does (and he also lowers Stability more). The best case scenario would be Yagoda getting purged late (if he gets purged at all), but as long as you can attempt to eliminate Stalin before Yezhov is purged, you're in the clear. Note that you can decline to purge Yezhov, but doing so will boost paranoia and cost you some political power."
  },
  {
   "k": "1l9n633",
   "en": "Anyways, what happens next also depends on who you want to save."
  },
  {
   "k": "13xxw43",
   "en": "If you are going to save the generals who get purged, rush towards ⟦0⟧.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "10bysdf",
   "en": "If you are planning on saving the advisors, take ⟦0⟧, and either ⟦1⟧ or ⟦2⟧ - you hypothetically have time for both, but the more time you waste, the more chance you risk of Stalin purging advisors who you otherwise would save. Afterwards, rush ⟦3⟧.",
   "frags": 4
  },
  {
   "k": "17i3q7o",
   "en": "Cooperation against Stalin"
  },
  {
   "k": "ppesq8",
   "en": "Liberate Ryuten"
  },
  {
   "k": "1x009bw",
   "en": "Regardless, as soon as Plan for the Coup is finished, immediately click the decision to sway the head of the NKVD to your cause, and take ⟦0⟧. It should finish before the NKVD head is swayed to your cause, so go straight for ⟦1⟧, and clicking the decision to eliminate Stalin. You have an 80% chance of Stalin dying this way - consider disbanding the entire army beforehand if he survives, since you'll likely get a civil war afterwards. However, if he does die, congrats! But you're not out of the woods just yet - Lavrentiy Beria, who will replace Stalin, will continue down the Centre path, and should he finish ⟦2⟧, the Bloodless coup option will be locked out, in which case, all your work will have been for naught. Afterwards, all that's left is to take either ⟦3⟧ or ⟦4⟧ to remove the Trotskyite Plot debuff.",
   "frags": 5
  },
  {
   "k": "g9b0ur",
   "en": "Dissent in the Party"
  },
  {
   "k": "h0zyiv",
   "en": "Coup 'etat"
  },
  {
   "k": "j7az7",
   "en": "Secure the Administration"
  },
  {
   "k": "b8kehf",
   "en": "The Enemies of the State"
  },
  {
   "k": "1rwhmq1",
   "en": "First Strike"
  },
  {
   "k": "18mepcl",
   "en": "One relatively easy way to deal with the Germany problem as the Soviet Union is to strike early and to strike hard. You start with nearly triple the manpower of Germany, though you have various debuffs and equipment shortages. Use your first political power to justify on Poland. Build military factories, run exercises to generate Army Experience, hire Chief of Army, switch to War Economy when your war support is over 50%. Update your infantry template to 9 infantry / 1 artillery + support artillery, engineers, recon, logistics. (You'll need to produce lots of artillery and supply equipment.) Disband units as necessary (in particularly, get rid of all NKVD Pogranichnaya Diviziya ASAP) to put 80-ish fully equipped infantry divisions on the Polish border. Likewise, disband some of your mechanized divisions. Declare on Poland, then start justifying on Germany. If all goes well, you'll capitulate Germany by early 1938, earning you \"We don't really like statistics\", \"The Revolution Triumphant\" (if you puppet some part of Germany instead of annexing it), a good shot at \"Not a Step Back\". (NOTE: you won't get \"One Step Forward\" unless you declare on Germany ⟦0⟧ you're still fighting Poland, which is risky.) Everyone will hate you (you'll generate 100% world tension all by yourself), and the UK will guarantee any Democratic country you attempt to justify on after Germany, but you'll be out of immediate danger. You can immediately follow by justifying on Austria (which, being unaligned/fascist, is fair game, as far as the UK is concerned), and then either justifying or using the \"Eradicate Western Fascism\" focus to attack Italy for \"The Pope? How many Divisions does he have?\". (Don't dawdle: with Germany gone and the Axis dismantled, the Allies may decide to invade and take Rome as early as 1940.)",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "3tl2ha",
   "en": "while"
  },
  {
   "k": "g9zvpt",
   "en": "Japan War"
  },
  {
   "k": "1bqa7hs",
   "en": "It's possible to drag Soviet Union into Sino-Japanese war, by guaranteeing ⟦0⟧ and ⟦1⟧. As such, when ⟦2⟧ invades China, Soviet Union ends up dragged in it. Don't forget to move divisions to ⟦3⟧ front beforehand in this case. Since you have more divisions (both if directly comparing USSR and Japan - and if factoring in puppets, China, and Volunteers from ⟦4⟧ and ⟦5⟧), and other Axis factions don't participate in this - defeating ⟦6⟧ should be rather easy. The only tricky part may be invasion of ⟦7⟧ mainland - as USSR's navy is small, and starts on opposite side of the globe.",
   "frags": 8
  },
  {
   "k": "eg9p8s",
   "en": "Italy"
  },
  {
   "k": "1pvokv",
   "en": "That can allow to remove debuffs sooner, protect ⟦0⟧, and defeat ⟦1⟧ if played correctly. ⟦2⟧, ⟦3⟧, ⟦4⟧, ⟦5⟧, ⟦6⟧, ⟦7⟧ can be turned into ⟦8⟧ - don't forget to take their industry and resources - though, China countries would also try to get parts of Japan during peace conference. You must also consider the time needed to move divisions to China and back - in case you're planning to bum-rush elsewhere, or otherwise go to war with someone.",
   "frags": 9
  },
  {
   "k": "11tcwh4",
